\title[The rational stars of the Berggren tree]{The rational stars of the Berggren tree:\\
charge quantisation, resolution, and a totient brightness law}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{11 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We give a complete arithmetic and metric analysis of the radial line structure that appears
when the Berggren ternary tree of primitive Pythagorean triples is embedded in the Poincar\'e
upper half-plane by the Euclid map $z(m,n) = (n+i)/m$. Rendered, the node set exhibits pencils
(``stars'') of straight lines emanating from certain boundary points --- conspicuously $0$ and
$1$, and more faintly near $0.2$, $0.33$, $0.5$ --- together with one distinguished ray into
the interior. Attached to each boundary rational $p/q$ in lowest terms and each node $z(m,n)$
is the \emph{star charge} $k = qn - pm$; the node lies on the Euclidean line
$\Rea z = p/q + (k/q)\Ima z$, which is a hypercycle at hyperbolic distance $\arsinh(|k|/q)$
from the geodesic over $p/q$, along which consecutive nodes have step lengths tending to $0$
and which converges to its ideal tip. We prove four exact laws: \textbf{quantisation} (if
$p,q$ are both odd every charge is odd; conversely every charge permitted by this parity
constraint is realised infinitely often, by an explicit $\mathrm{SL}_2(\ZZ)$ construction);
\textbf{resolution} (nodes on distinct rays have $\sinh$-widths differing by at least
$\delta(p/q) = \gap(p,q)/q$ with $\gap = 1$ or $2$ according to parity, attained; whence the
rationals of $[0,1]$ with $\delta \ge 2/5$ are exactly $0,\tfrac15,\tfrac13,\tfrac12,\tfrac35,1$,
and only finitely many exceed any threshold); \textbf{brightness} (a window of $2k$ ray
parameters carries $2\varphi(k)$ nodes, or $\varphi(k) = \varphi(2k)$ in one mixed regime);
and \textbf{rationality of stars} (two nodes collinear with an irrational ideal point
coincide). We add the metric background --- the radial law
$\cosh d(i,z(m,n)) = (m^2+n^2+1)/(2m)$, the step-length trichotomy separating the parabolic fan
orbits from the hyperbolic Pell spine of step $\log(1+\sqrt2)$, and the ball count
$\Theta(e^{2R})$ --- and derive a no-free-lunch theorem: a hypotenuse collision witnessing a
factorisation of $N$ with extracted divisor $g$ has its two witnesses at distance at least
$\log g - \log 2$, while the smallest ball guaranteed to contain both already holds
$\Theta(N)$ nodes.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{Pythagorean triples, Berggren tree, Poincar\'e half-plane, hypercycle, star charge,
